# Append-only JSONL files use git's built-in union merge driver so concurrent
# branches that each append their own rows can be rebased without manual
# conflict resolution.
#
# The `union` driver concatenates both sides of a conflict instead of marking
# them. This is correct because:
#   1. These files declare themselves append-only (per their `_meta` row).
#   2. Each row is independent — order matters only by timestamp, not by
#      adjacency.
#   3. Deletions by either side would be silently lost, but our policy
#      forbids deletions anyway (codex bot enforces this on review).
#
# Rationale: with 14+ parallel PRs all appending one row each, the previous
# "default merge" behaviour produced a continuous merge-storm where every
# advance of `dev` re-conflicted ~13 other branches against a single
# append-only file. Switching to `merge=union` makes the conflict structural
# noise disappear at the git layer.
#
# Scope: only files whose schema is genuinely append-only-list-of-rows.
# Do NOT add `merge=union` for files with structural cross-line invariants
# (e.g. registry indices keyed by id where duplicates would corrupt state).
evidence/audit-chain.jsonl merge=union
registry/fixuptasks.jsonl merge=union
